% ============================================================================
% rock-type-formation-component — for two of the three basic rock types, the
% INDIVIDUAL material(s) or agent(s) an already-cited USGS sentence lists as
% forming it, decoded one-per-row (adj-facts-stdlib, GEOLOGY).
% ============================================================================
% A sibling to the already-shipped `rock-type.adj`
% (`rock_type(rock, formation_process)`, the process by which each of the
% three basic rock classes forms — igneous → crystallized_molten_rock,
% sedimentary → deposited_weathered_material, metamorphic →
% heat_and_pressure_transformation). Two of that table's own three per-row
% USGS quotes each list MORE than one material/agent, joined by an
% exhaustive "or" or comma-separated list — a fact the single
% `formation_process` atom folded into one compound label, with no room to
% decode each listed item as its own queryable fact:
%
%   sedimentary (two alternatives joined by "or"):
%     "Sedimentary rocks are formed from pre-existing rocks or pieces of
%      once-living organisms."
%
%   metamorphic (three agents, comma-listed then "or"):
%     "Metamorphic rocks form when rocks are subjected to high heat, high
%      pressure, hot mineral-rich fluids or, more commonly, some
%      combination of these factors."
%     (the trailing "some combination of these factors" is the source's
%      own meta-commentary about the list, not a fourth named agent, so it
%      is not carried as a row)
%
% Recall is a binding query:
%
%     ? rock_type_formation_component(metamorphic, $Component)
%       % high_heat, high_pressure, AND hot_mineral_rich_fluids (three answers)
%
% This is a native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5, a TWO-column table): each `row`
% lowers to a ground relation `rock_type_formation_component(rock,
% component)` carrying the citation, so the lookup above is the ordinary
% SLD binding query — the engine returns each component WITH its source, on
% the CPU, with zero model calls, and abstains on a rock type whose own
% cited span names only ONE material with no listed alternative.
% `igneous` is deliberately NOT a row here: its own already-quoted span
% ("hot, molten rock crystallizes and solidifies") names exactly one
% material — no enumerated "or"/list of alternatives to decode — so this
% sibling is HONESTLY NARROWER than its parent, grounding exactly the two
% rock types whose cited spans list more than one component and abstaining
% on the third, or any other word, rather than inventing one. The query
% also runs BACKWARD — bind a component and recall which rock type(s) it
% forms:
%
%     ? rock_type_formation_component($Rock, hot_mineral_rich_fluids)
%       % metamorphic
%
% Provenance: reproduces, byte-for-byte, the SAME two USGS sentences
% `rock-type.adj`'s own header already quotes in full for its sedimentary
% and metamorphic rows — no new WebFetch, only a different schema decoding
% each listed component as its own row instead of folding the whole clause
% into one compound `formation_process` atom. An ADJ `table` carries ONE
% provenance envelope, so `source`/`locator`/`trust` below hold the
% sedimentary span (matching `rock-type.adj`'s own convention of listing
% each row's distinct USGS page in prose); the metamorphic row's own span
% and locator are listed above for independent auditability. `trust
% authoritative` — the same tier `rock-type.adj` already carries (USGS, a
% primary U.S. government science resource).
% (See ../README.md; feedback_nothing_human_authored,
% feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts.)
% ============================================================================

table rock_type_formation_component {
    columns rock, component

    row (sedimentary, pre_existing_rocks)
    row (sedimentary, pieces_of_once_living_organisms)
    row (metamorphic, high_heat)
    row (metamorphic, high_pressure)
    row (metamorphic, hot_mineral_rich_fluids)

    source "Sedimentary rocks are formed from pre-existing rocks or pieces of once-living organisms."
    locator "https://www.usgs.gov/faqs/what-are-sedimentary-rocks"
    trust authoritative

    cites "Metamorphic rocks form when rocks are subjected to high heat, high pressure, hot mineral-rich fluids or, more commonly, some combination of these factors." locator "https://www.usgs.gov/faqs/what-are-metamorphic-rocks"
}
